# Z80 breadboard computer — standalone circuit.
#
# The bench is the deliverable here: Z80 + RAM + ROM + MC6850 with
# 74HC00 decode, wired like the real build, and bw-board's
# extractZ80Machine turns exactly this wiring into the machine config
# (memory regions + the I/O-mapped ACIA).
#
# This bench stays program-less on purpose, but NOT for the reason that
# used to be written here. A DEVICE Z80 program axis now exists — see the
# sibling `z80-pd-bench`, which has a real walking-light program on it.
#
# The axis simply does not reach THIS board. sb3Creator's `z80Hw()` maps
# `OUT0-7` / `IN0-7` onto a 74HC374 write latch and a 74HC244 read buffer
# at I/O port 0, and this circuit has neither: its parts are the Z80, a
# 28C256 ROM, a 62256 RAM, a 74HC00 for decode, and an MC6850 — and
# bw-board's extractZ80Machine accordingly reports one port here,
# `acia6850 at 0x80`. The console is a serial ACIA, and the emitter has no
# serial axis for the Z80, so there is no pin for a program to drive.
#
# That is the honest state: the bench is the deliverable, verified against
# the bus extractor by check-extract.mjs. A DEVICE line here would still
# only teach the gallery to lie — it would just be lying about a different
# thing than it used to.
